# Skill-citation baseline — backticked skill names cited here that are NOT a skill
# directory in this repo, with what each one actually is.
#
# Read by check_skill_routes.py. One `name  # reason` per line. Any backticked
# kebab-case name (3+ hyphen-words) cited in a SKILL.md, README.md or
# CONTRIBUTING.md that is neither a skill directory here nor listed below is a hard
# CI failure.
#
# WHY THIS FILE IS THE FIX AND NOT AN EXCUSE. Issue #39 measured 51 names cited as
# if a reader could go and open them, across 77 sites in 41 files, mostly in
# "Sister skill:" and "See also:" lines — which is precisely where a reader goes
# when the current skill did not answer their question. A gate alone could not ship:
# with 51 live offenders it would start red and be muted inside a week, and a muted
# gate is worse than no gate because it looks like coverage. So the names are
# recorded here ONCE, with what is actually known about each, and the gate then
# fails only on a NEW one. That is the same design as `.release-parity-accepted`.
#
# The reader-facing half is that this file is now the map: a name in a skill body
# that you cannot find is in here, with its status and where to get it if anywhere.
#
# HOW IT WAS ESTABLISHED (2026-08-12), two independent checks, both with a positive
# control that correctly found the one known deleted skill:
#   · `git log --all -- '*<name>*'` and a sweep of every path in every ref — none of
#     these ever existed as a path in this repo's history, so none is recoverable
#     with `git show <sha>^:<path>` and none can be retargeted against what a dropped
#     skill actually said;
#   · every public repo under the author's account, plus the public `superpowers`
#     bundle — 9 names resolve there and are marked PUBLISHED with the repo.
#
# THE RULE FOR NEW WORK. If you cite a name that is not a skill directory here, add
# it below with its status in the same commit. Do not add a name to make a red gate
# green without checking what it is: a confident wrong note is worse than an admitted
# gap, because the note is what the next person trusts instead of re-checking.

